{"name":"Pavlova","hex":"#baab87","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Pavlova","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A transliteration of the Russian surname Па́влова (Pávlova), the feminine variant of Pavlov.\n\nAnna Pavlova (Russian ballet dancer).","Anna Pavlova (Russian ballet dancer)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of pavlova."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Pavlova"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pavlova","blocks":[{"text":"Pavlova is a meringue-based dessert. Originating in either Australia or New Zealand in the early 20th century, it was named after the Russian ballerina Anna Pavlova. Taking the form of a cake-like circular block of baked meringue, pavlova has a crisp crust and soft, light inside. The confection is usually topped with fruit and whipped cream. The name is commonly pronounced pav-LOH-və or pahv-LOH-və, and occasionally closer to the name of the dancer, as PAHV-lə-və."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Pavlova_dessert.JPG/330px-Pavlova_dessert.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavlova","wikidata":"Q1419824"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Citrus_Pavlova_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_restaurant.jpg/500px-Citrus_Pavlova_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_restaurant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Citrus_Pavlova_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_restaurant.jpg/1280px-Citrus_Pavlova_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_restaurant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Citrus_Pavlova_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_restaurant.jpg/500px-Citrus_Pavlova_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_restaurant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu